repository-code: https://github.com/MSKazemi/m100-silicon-binning
url: https://mskazemi.com/m100-silicon-binning/
abstract: >-
  Processor vendors sell partially defective dies by permanently fusing off the
  faulty units, and the resulting per-die maps of which units were disabled are
  proprietary. This work shows that the out-of-band sensor telemetry
  supercomputers already collect discloses them: a baseboard management
  controller exposes one thermal sensor per physical core position and does not
  renumber survivors, so the set of reporting sensors is the harvest map. Maps
  are recovered for all 1,962 POWER9 sockets of CINECA's Marconi100 from a
  public dataset and tracked across 1.67 million socket-days. Harvesting
  operates exactly at the POWER9 slice with zero exceptions and is per-die
  rather than per-SKU; a thermal probe independently recovers the sensor index
  as the physical ordering of slices on the die; and a sharp procurement
  boundary separates two lots with different binning statistics.
keywords:
